    Only entries whose pronunciation Wiktionary's UK list (via WikiPron) also gives are
    kept; Wiktionary is used as a check and none of its text is included.

    Provenance, as stated by Britfone's author (README and issue #2 of that repository,
    2023-10-23): the transcriptions started from CMUdict; Wiktionary, Wikipedia and the
    Collins, Oxford, Cambridge and Macmillan dictionaries are named as further sources for
    British forms; the dictionaries "were consulted rather than included", and the only
    sources included, "after heavy modification", were computer-readable ones "(like
    CMUDICT and BNC)". polyg2p relies on that statement and on the MIT licence above; it
    has not audited Britfone entry by entry. The file can be emptied down to its header
    line without touching the code: en-gb then runs on its rules and
    data/en_brit_words.tsv alone (see docs/english-accents.md for what
    that costs in accuracy).

data/en_gb_func.tsv, data/en_brit_words.tsv
    Short lists of British function-word and word forms written by the polyg2p authors.

data/it_suffix.tsv, data/it_open.tsv (Italian stress / vowel-openness statistics)
    Aggregate counts computed from WikiPron's Italian data, which is extracted
    from Wiktionary (CC BY-SA). The tables hold only per-ending statistics, not
    pronunciations; see "Data licensing" in README.md before redistributing.

